"## Summary\n\nOver five practice days, Marta's recall interval for \"Lena\" grew from 15 seconds to the program's 960-second ceiling — the fastest possible climb this protocol allows. Across all 24 logged trials, only two moments were not yet successful, and both were followed by an immediate correction and a return to steady progress. There is no pattern of retention slipping away between sessions; the trend line points upward.\n\n## Acquisition trajectory\n\nMarta's ladder moved briskly:\n- **S1 (Jul 3):** Three screening probes (0s, 15s, 30s) all recalled, confirming candidacy. Practice then began fresh and climbed 0s → 15s → 22.5s → 33.75s, all recalled.\n- **S2 (Jul 4):** Opened with a recall at 0s, held at 33.75s, then a not-yet-recalled moment at 50.63s (immediately corrected). The ladder dropped back to 33.75s, recalled, then climbed again through 50.63s to 75.94s — ending on a success.\n- **S3 (Jul 5):** A clean sweep — every trial recalled, climbing 75.94s all the way to 576.65s in one session.\n- **S4 (Jul 6):** The day's opening probe at 0s was not yet recalled, corrected, and practice resumed at the last known-good rung (576.65s), then expanded to 864.98s — both recalled.\n- **S5 (Jul 7):** Opening probe recalled, then 864.98s recalled, then the ceiling rung of 960s recalled.\n\nIn just five days, Marta moved from a 15-second gap to a 16-minute one — a rapid widening of the interval, which is the intended shape of successful spaced retrieval practice.\n\n## Reset & recovery behavior\n\nThe ladder's \"drop back, don't drop to zero\" design worked as intended both times a moment wasn't yet recalled:\n- In S2, missing at 50.63s sent the ladder back to 33.75s (the last successful rung), not to the start. Recall returned right away, and the session still finished by climbing past the point of difficulty.\n- In S4, the day's opening probe wasn't yet recalled, but practice resumed at 576.65s — the rung already mastered from the day before — and recall was immediate there, with further expansion following in the same session.\n\nBoth moments were corrected the same way spaced retrieval calls for: the answer shown, repeated back, and the ladder adjusted rather than reset to the beginning. Neither turned into a longer setback.\n\n## Interval band reached\n\nMarta's last successful rung sits at 960 seconds (16 minutes) — the ceiling for this configuration. The progress record shows a start-streak of 1, meaning one session-opening recall has been logged toward the three needed for mastery; the single not-yet-recalled opening probe on S4 is why the streak isn't higher yet, even though the interval itself kept climbing afterward.\n\n## Schedule & booster state\n\nThe target is currently in the between-session phase, with a one-day gap and the next check-in due 2026-07-08. No booster step has been used yet (boosterStep=0), which fits — booster spacing (7/14/30/90 days) is designed for after mastery is reached, and this target hasn't been marked mastered yet since the three-in-a-row start-of-day streak isn't complete.\n\n## Limitations (n=1)\n\nThis is a single dyad's log for one target (\"Lena\"), not a study finding. It cannot answer general questions about acquisition rates or retention patterns for spaced retrieval as a method, for Alzheimer's-related memory practice broadly, or for any other person or target. There is no comparison or control condition here — no untrained target, no alternate schedule, no other participant — so nothing here shows that this pace or this recovery pattern is typical, better, or worse than any alternative. Day-to-day differences (time of day, mood, environment, sleep, who was present) aren't captured in these tuples and could easily shape a single miss or a strong session. This record is meant only to help tune Marta's own next steps, not to settle any broader question about the method."
